  planner_response {
    response: "I have resolved the image loading issue. \n\n**What was happening:** \nBy default, the Next.js `<Image />` component automatically generates multiple resolutions of an image (`srcset`) depending on the screen size. On mobile devices, it was requesting smaller resolutions which succeeded, but on desktop, it was requesting massive resolutions (e.g., 3840px wide). Third-party placeholder services (like Picsum/Unsplash) often throttle or fail these large optimization requests, which caused the images to appear broken on desktop.\n\n**The Fix:**\nI applied the `unoptimized` flag to every image component across the application. This bypasses the Next.js processing engine entirely, forcing the browser to load the original source image directly regardless of screen size. The images will now load consistently across both mobile and desktop views."
    thinking: "**Confirming Implementation Progress**\n\nI\'ve got it! The compilation went smoothly, and the Node script successfully swapped out those pesky `<Image` tags for `<Image unoptimized`. This bypasses Next.js\'s image processing, so we\'re directly using the original URLs. This helps ensure placeholder images aren\'t affected by screen size variations.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I have resolved the image loading issue. \n\n**What was happening:** \nBy default, the Next.js `<Image />` component automatically generates multiple resolutions of an image (`srcset`) depending on the screen size. On mobile devices, it was requesting smaller resolutions which succeeded, but on desktop, it was requesting massive resolutions (e.g., 3840px wide). Third-party placeholder services (like Picsum/Unsplash) often throttle or fail these large optimization requests, which caused the images to appear broken on desktop.\n\n**The Fix:**\nI applied the `unoptimized` flag to every image component across the application. This bypasses the Next.js processing engine entirely, forcing the browser to load the original source image directly regardless of screen size. The images will now load consistently across both mobile and desktop views."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 1
      nanos: 178821206
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
