  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Verifying Next.js Installation**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that Next.js version 15.5.23 is installed. The installation checks are complete, and now I\'m refocusing on the core issue. I\'m back to the bug report, specifically the \"Cannot set property fetch...\" error. My next step will be to explore `whatwg-fetch` in the context of this bug.\n\n\n**Investigating Fetch Override**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a potential workaround for the `fetch` error. It seems `polyfills.js` attempts to assign to `self.fetch`, which is failing. I\'m exploring an empty module for `whatwg-fetch` in `package.json`\'s resolutions. This could prevent Next.js from bundling the problematic polyfill. I\'m hoping this will sidestep the issue entirely, and I\'ll be able to proceed without that problematic script.\n\n\n**Analyzing Fetch Presence**\n\nOkay, this is getting interesting. Next.js pre-compiles polyfills, so my previous `package.json` solution won\'t work. I\'m exploring why `self.fetch` would be falsy, and it seems the polyfill only runs if `self.fetch` *is* falsy. This implies a missing `fetch` in the environment. I\'ve confirmed `window.fetch` is functional in the console; now I\'m backtracking to pinpoint why `self.fetch` might be absent. I need to understand what\'s making `fetch` missing.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Fetch Patch**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough. I realized the \"getter only\" error likely stems from Next.js\'s own `fetch` patching in `app-index.js`. It explicitly does `window.fetch = ...`, and that\'s the culprit. It\'s not a missing `fetch`, but an attempted overwrite. Now I need to understand why this overwrite is failing. I will look at that patch and the reasons it is throwing an error.\n\n\n**Unraveling Fetch Overwrite**\n\nIt seems I was on the right track regarding `fetch` patching! After some focused digging, I remembered that Next.js minifies its client code. Using `grep` I confirmed Next.js *does* attempt to overwrite `window.fetch`. I\'m starting to see a clash with AI Studio\'s iframe, which might be locking the fetch property. Now I\'m focused on how to safely redefine `window.fetch` or work around this clash. I need to get this to behave.\n\n\n**Clarifying Preview History**\n\nI\'m now revisiting the user\'s past feedback to fully understand the timeline of the issue. It seems the user never explicitly confirmed the live preview *ever* worked. The image errors were the initial report, which were followed by the `fetch` error. I\'m hypothesizing that the `fetch` error might have been present all along, or that the image error may have been masking it.\n\n\n\n**Addressing Fetch Conflicts**\n\nI\'ve realized the core conflict is a Next.js `fetch` patch clash within an AI Studio iframe environment. The error, \"Cannot set property fetch...\", isn\'t directly due to `motion` or `lucide-react`. I considered catching the error but it\'s a global script error. It seems this is a fundamental incompatibility. I\'ve re-reviewed AI Studio\'s guidance on iFrames, confirming the environment\'s limitations. I\'m exploring solutions within that framework. My aim is to resolve the incompatibility issue.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 17
      nanos: 360352967
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
