  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Clarifying Dependencies**\n\nOkay, I\'ve had a breakthrough! Remember that `npm ls --all` search for `whatwg-fetch`? Turns out it\'s a sub-dependency of `firebase-tools`, which is a `devDependency`. So, the `whatwg-fetch` polyfill isn\'t bundled into our client code, it\'s just in `polyfills`. This changes everything!\n\n\n**Investigating Global Patching**\n\nI\'ve been examining the potential of Next.js globally patching `fetch`. I found that Next.js *does* indeed patch `window.fetch` to add caching behavior, using a pattern like `window.fetch = function fetch(...) { ... }`. My earlier search in `main-app.js` didn\'t turn up any such patching but this confirms it\'s possible. This might be the source of the `getter` error.\n\n\n**Exploring Iframe Restrictions**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the AI Studio iframe. It seems `window.fetch` *might* be locked within the iframe environment, as opposed to the application\'s core code. Opening the app in a new tab reveals `window.fetch` isn\'t locked there. The iframe\'s sandbox restrictions are the likely culprit, potentially stemming from the `allow-scripts` attribute or host-injected wrappers. Can I simply advise the user to open in a new tab? Or should I still pursue a code workaround, even if it\'s futile given a non-configurable `window.fetch`? I\'ll retest in a new tab to confirm.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 6
      nanos: 376162993
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
