  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Next.js Errors**\n\nI\'ve been examining the error messages from Next.js. Specifically, I\'m focusing on the compilation errors related to route definitions, namely how Next.js handles optional catch-all routes. I think I\'ve identified that the issue surfaces when the compiler encounters conflicting route specificities.\n\n\n**Investigating Browser Errors**\n\nI\'m now investigating a TypeError in the browser: \"Cannot set property fetch\". This stems from a getter-only `window.fetch`. I\'m exploring the possibility of redefining `window.fetch` using a configurable approach, but I\'m checking to see if that\'s even possible in `app/layout.tsx`. I think I\'m starting to see how the user\'s setup might be able to cause this error.\n\n\n**Refining Fetch Redefinition**\n\nI\'m revisiting the `window.fetch` issue. The previous fix used `Object.defineProperty` but might have been too aggressive. I\'m focusing on scenarios where `fetch` is *apparently* read-only, yet possibly configurable. This means that a setter might be technically possible even when it initially seems blocked, and that the previous code was too naive. I\'m trying to refine the logic to handle this edge case more robustly in `app/layout.tsx`.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 5
      nanos: 628938943
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
