  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Addressing the Fetch Error**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the `fetch` error. I\'ve pinpointed the `Uncaught TypeError` in `polyfills.js`, specifically related to Next.js\'s injection with `nomodule`. I\'m exploring a straightforward fix for the error, as my primary objective is to resolve it as expediently as possible.\n\n\n**Investigating the Fetch Origin**\n\nI\'m now digging into the root cause, wondering why the user\'s Chrome browser is executing `polyfills.js` when it shouldn\'t. Despite the `nomodule` attribute, the error persists. I\'m exploring the possibility of another `whatwg-fetch` instance in the main bundle, suspecting a transitive dependency. I\'ve eliminated direct imports of `whatwg-fetch`, but a nested dependency could be the culprit.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Root Cause**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough. It appears the issue stems from Next.js 15.x\'s `fetch` monkeypatching. Specifically, it attempts to redefine `window.fetch`, which is restricted in the AI Studio environment, leading to the \"Cannot set property fetch\" error. I\'m exploring a potential workaround involving temporarily overriding `Object.defineProperty` within my application\'s layout to circumvent this restriction.\n\n\n**Exploring Patching Workarounds**\n\nI\'m now considering the implications of Next.js\'s direct assignment of `window.fetch`. I\'ve realized that if that assignment fails, the entire application will halt. I\'m exploring different avenues, like whether the getter could be manipulated to include a setter function, but I can\'t see how that would be possible in JS. I\'m also investigating if it is possible to configure Next.js to not patch fetch, considering it is for the client side cache. Finally, as a last resort, I\'m now considering the feasibility of reverting to the `pages/` directory structure as a workaround.\n\n\n**Revisiting Version Compatibility**\n\nI\'m now circling back to that build error, pondering why I got a `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null` related to `useContext`. The issue appeared after I messed with `whatwg-fetch`. I\'m verifying the versions of `react` and `react-dom` against Next.js 15.x requirements. They are version 19, which should work. I\'m recalling that I emptied and restored `node_modules/whatwg-fetch/fetch.js`, so I\'ll see if that messed things up.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 989413343
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
